`Process(cmd).!` is the most common way to launch a process in scala. It is
companion-apply sugar for `scala.sys.process.Process.apply(cmd)`, and the scala
analyzer emits it under the name `Process`, which no catalogue row carried. On
sbt, 6 of 6 explicit-import launches classified as nothing. The module still
counted as examined there, because uncatalogued `exitValue` / `destroy` calls
satisfy the starvation proxy. So a `{subprocess, must_not_exist}` claim found no
chain.

FIX, shape (b) of the row: the catalogue rows the name the analyzer emits.
- `module: scala.sys.process.Process` gains `functions: [apply, Process]` under
  both simultaneous boundaries (fs_write, subprocess). This is the
  constructor-named-row convention swift.yaml uses (NWListener, ServerBootstrap).
- `apply` is re-kinded to function: it is called on the companion object
  (ADR-0059). Its ledger entry is removed, so the ledger goes 30 -> 29.
- `run` stays a method row. No change in scope here.
- `scala.sys.process.Process.Process` joins HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES. The
  subprocess drift guard caught its absence.

MEASURED. 11 scala repos surveyed at 944735a08f: sbt, tapir, lila,
playframework, pekko, spark, scala3, gatling, http4s, cats, zio. Each survey's
unresolved scala call edges were classified under three catalogues, and the
edge SETS were diffed (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/narij_09242026/AB.txt).
- B1 (`Process` in ambiguous_names): +14 over A.
- B2 (`Process` not listed): +22 over A.
- Both: 0 lost, 0 changed. The controls (gatling, http4s, cats, zio) and lila
  do not move.
- B2's 8 extra edges are bare `external:Process`, how `import
  scala.sys.process._` / `.*` arrives. Each was read in source: all 8 are
  genuine scala.sys.process launches.
- The rule was fixed before measuring: B2 only if every bare match is genuine.
  B2 it is, and the choice and its fallback are written into the yaml note and
  a test.
- tapir, playframework and pekko stop reporting scala.sys.process.Process as
  starved.

